## Architecture of a High-Performance Game Server

When handling thousands of concurrent real-time player connections with rigid tick rate guarantees (e.g., 60 Hz = 16.6ms per frame), blocking I/O and thread-per-client models fall apart under context switching overhead.

### The Single-Threaded Event Loop + Worker Pool Pattern

Using **Boost.Asio / standalone Asio** with modern C++20 coroutines (`co_await`) lets us write linear, synchronous-looking networking code while executing completely asynchronously over `epoll` (Linux) or `kqueue` (macOS).

```cpp
#include <asio.hpp>
#include <memory>
#include <iostream>

class Session : public std::enable_shared_from_this<Session> {
public:
    explicit Session(asio::ip::tcp::socket socket) : socket_(std::move(socket)) {}

    void start() {
        readHeader();
    }

private:
    void readHeader() {
        auto self(shared_from_this());
        asio::async_read(socket_, asio::buffer(headerBuffer_),
            [this, self](std::error_code ec, std::size_t length) {
                if (!ec) {
                    processPacket();
                }
            });
    }

    void processPacket() {
        // Zero-copy deserialization and tick enqueue
    }

    asio::ip::tcp::socket socket_;
    std::array<uint8_t, 8> headerBuffer_{};
};
```

---

## Packet Framing & Memory Arena Pools

In TCP streams, messages are byte streams without message boundaries. We implement a lightweight 4-byte header:
- `uint16_t packet_id`
- `uint16_t payload_size`

By pre-allocating memory arenas for active sessions, we eliminate heap allocations during real-time match gameplay, ensuring sub-5ms packet turnaround.
